          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong Freyd cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathcal{T}}
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            along the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section+functor"} global section functor
          %p
            As
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathcal{T}}
            comes equipped with two well-behaved projection functors the resulting category has many good logical properties making the construction an important tool in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            and theoretical computer science.
          %p
            The Freyd cover is sometimes known as the
            %strong Sierpinski cone
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong scone
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            it behaves similarly to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
            on a space, but with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,1]
            replaced by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+space"} Sierpinski space
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            be a category with a terminal object and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝒯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = \mathcal{T} (1, -)
            the global section functor . The
            %strong Freyd cover
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            is the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathcal{T}}
            whose objects are triples
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \xi, U)
            where:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
