            %p
              preserves limits, because the contravariant power set functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 2
                      %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P = 2^-
              is monadic. But it is easily checked that this composite is the contravariant representable given by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (2, \mathbf{1}, 2 \to \Gamma(\mathbf{1}))
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              The terminal object in the initial topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+object"} connected
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+object"} projective
              in the sense that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo =
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = \hom(1, -) \colon \mathcal{T} \to Set
              preserves finite colimits.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p We divide the argument into three segments:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  The hom-functor preserves finite limits, so by general properties of Artin gluing, the Freyd cover
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathcal{T}}
                  is also a topos. Observe that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
                  is equivalent to the slice
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathcal{T}}/M
                  where
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                  is the object
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ∅
                          %mo ,
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mi ∅
                          %mo →
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\emptyset, \mathbf{1}, \emptyset \to \Gamma(\mathbf{1}))
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \colon \widehat{\mathcal{T}} \to \mathcal{T}
                %p
                  Since
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
                  is initial,
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                  is a retraction for the unique logical functor
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo →
                          %mover
                            %mi 𝒯
                            %mo ^
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon \mathcal{T} \to \widehat{\mathcal{T}}
              %li
                %p
                  We have maps
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}(1, -) \to \widehat{\mathcal{T}}(i 1, i-) \cong \widehat{\mathcal{T}}(1, i-)
                  (the isomorphism comes from
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ≅
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i 1 \cong 1
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  is logical), and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo ^
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi 𝒯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi 𝒯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathcal{T}}(1, i-) \to \mathcal{T}(\pi 1, \pi i-) \cong \mathcal{T}(1, -)
                  since
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
                  is logical and retracts
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒯
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}(1, -)
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %p
              Finally, since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}(1, -)
              is a retract of a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathcal{T}}(1, i-)
              that preserves finite colimits (by the lemma, and the fact that the logical functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              preserves finite colimits), it must also preserve finite colimits.
          %p
            This is important because it implies that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of the free topos (which is exactly “intuitionistic higher-order logic”) satisfies the following properties:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em disjunction property
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/or"} or
                Q” is provable in the empty
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em existence property
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in A
                such that
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi P
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+element"} global element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\colon 1\to A
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x)
                is provable in the empty context. (Again, this is clearly a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructivity
                property.)
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em negation property
            %li
              %p
                All numerals in the free topos are “standard”, i.e., the global sections functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo =
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi 𝒯
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma = \hom(1, -): \mathcal{T} \to Set
                preserves the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                (because
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                can be characterized in terms of finite colimits and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %h3#AsALocalTopos As a local topos
          %p
            The Freyd cover of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+topos"} local topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retract"} retract
            of a Freyd cover.
          %p
            See (
            %a{:href => "#Johnstone"}> Johnstone, lemma C3.6.4
            ).
          %h3#as_a_fibration As a fibration
          %p
            The Freyd cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi 𝒯
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mathcal{T}}
            is fibered over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{T}
            as it arises equivalently by change of base of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codomain+fibration"} codomain fibration
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            along the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+section+functor"} global section functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %p
            See (
            %a{:href => "#Jacobs"}> Jacobs, p.57
            ).
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+gluing"} Artin gluing
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sierpinski+topos"} Sierpinski topos
          %h2#references References
          %p Some of the above material is taken from
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"}> Tom Leinster
              ,
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/12136/freyd-cover-of-a-category"} reply at MathOverflow
          %p The following two n-café blog posts provide an introductory discussion of the scone construction from a geometric and a logical perspective:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                %em Discreteness, Concreteness, Fibrations, and Scones
                , November 2011. (
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2011/11/discreteness_concreteness_fibr.html"}> link
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                %em Scones, Logical Relations, and Parametricity
                , April 2013. (
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2013/04/scones_logical_relations_and_p.html"}> link
                )
          %p
            You can find more on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin+gluing"} Artin gluing
            in this important (and nice) paper:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aurelio+Carboni"}> Aurelio Carboni
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"} Peter Johnstone
              %em Connected limits, familial representability and Artin glueing
              , Mathematical Structures in Computer Science
              %strong 5
              (1995) pp.441-459.
          %p plus
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aurelio+Carboni"}> Aurelio Carboni
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"} Peter Johnstone
              %em Corrigenda to ‘Connected limits…’
              , Mathematical Structures in Computer Science
              %strong 14
              (2004) pp.185-187.
          %p See also section C3.6 of
          %ul
            %li
              %p#Johnstone
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
                  vol. 2
                \. Oxford UP 2002.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bart+Jacobs"}> Bart Jacobs
                ,
                %em Categorical Logic and Type Theory
                , Elsevier Amsterdam 1999.
          %p The following paper studies logical properties of the Freyd cover:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
              ,
              %em On the Freyd Cover of a Topos
              , Notre Dame J. Formal Logic
              %strong 24
              no.4 (1983) pp.517-526. (
              %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/download/pdf_1/euclid.ndjfl/1093870454"}> pdf
              )
          %p The argument given above for the properties of the free topos is an amplification of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> P. Freyd
              , A. Scedrov,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories%2C+Allegories"} Categories, Allegories
              , North-Holland Amsterdam 1990. (1.(10)31, p.192)
          %p For more on the free topos and the first appearance in print of Freyd’s observation see
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"}> J. Lambek
              , P. J. Scott,
              %em Intuitionist Type Theory and the Free Topos
              , JPAA
              %strong 19
              (1980) pp.215-257.
          %p
            The following studies when the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+map+classifier"} partial map classifier
            is a Sierpiński cone in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+domain+theory"} synthetic domain theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Sterling"}> Jonathan Sterling
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Baby steps in higher domain theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://nyuad.nyu.edu/en/events/2024/april/homotopy-type-theory-and-computing.html"} Homotopy Type Theory and Computing – Classical and Quantum
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Center+for+Quantum+and+Topological+Systems"} Center for Quantum and Topological Systems
                (
                %a{:href => "https://cdnapisec.kaltura.com/html5/html5lib/v2.73.2/mwEmbedFrame.php/p/1674401/uiconf_id/23435151/entry_id/1_b57uwin3?wid=_1674401&iframeembed=true&playerId=kaltura_player&entry_id=1_b57uwin3"}> video
                )
            %li#PS25
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leoni+Pugh"}> Leoni Pugh
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Sterling"} Jonathan Sterling
                %em When is the partial map classifier a Sierpiński cone?
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.06789"}> arXiv:2504.06789
                )
